Just somewhere where the rest of us do not have to watch you die, and then tell

1505
01:28:27.152 --> 01:28:29.552
me that this is about public safety.

1506
01:28:29.592 --> 01:28:31.852
Tell me this is about protecting everyday people.

1507
01:28:31.892 --> 01:28:35.672
Each time I hear it, the definition seems to shrink because there are

1508
01:28:35.732 --> 01:28:39.131
no everyday people. There are only people.

1509
01:28:39.692 --> 01:28:43.332
People who were once children before this country turned them into a

1510
01:28:43.392 --> 01:28:47.372
statistic. And when politicians fail the people, they have a habit of

1511
01:28:47.392 --> 01:28:51.212
describing them as criminals, and nothing terrifies a failing policy

1512
01:28:51.252 --> 01:28:53.892
more than the people forced to survive it.

1513
01:28:54.192 --> 01:28:57.572
So I'll keep asking you all the question this bill keeps stepping

1514
01:28:57.652 --> 01:28:59.072
around. Move on?

1515
01:28:59.892 --> 01:29:03.712
Move on where? And I will not be taking any questions. Thank you.

1516
01:29:05.452 --> 01:29:07.292
Thank you very much for your submission.

1517
01:29:07.892 --> 01:29:08.332
Thank you.

1518
01:29:08.672 --> 01:29:10.552
We are now heading onto

1519
01:29:11.372 --> 01:29:12.752
Pillar New Zealand,

1520
01:29:13.732 --> 01:29:16.112
and we'll just wait for them to come online.

1521
01:29:22.272 --> 01:29:24.112
I think it's Nathan and Aryan.

1522
01:29:28.632 --> 01:29:29.392
Good morning. Can you hear me?

1523
01:29:29.852 --> 01:29:33.412
Oh, welcome, Aryan. Welcome to the Justice Committee.

1524
01:29:33.632 --> 01:29:35.392
We've got you for 10 minutes today.

1525
01:29:35.772 --> 01:29:38.241
Do we have a colleague as well coming online?

1526
01:29:38.272 --> 01:29:39.812
It'll just be me this morning.

1527
01:29:40.132 --> 01:29:43.092
Okay. Welcome indeed. Please leave us some time.

1528
01:29:43.812 --> 01:29:47.652
I'll probably let you know with about eight minutes gone, and if there's

1529
01:29:47.662 --> 01:29:50.212
any questions pop up, I'll let you know as well. Thank you.

1530
01:29:50.812 --> 01:29:54.092
Well, thank you for the opportunity to speak to this legislation.

1531
01:29:54.172 --> 01:29:57.542
I won't be as probably poetic and beautifully presented as the last one,

1532
01:29:58.332 --> 01:30:00.452
but I hope it's still of value.

1533
01:30:01.412 --> 01:30:03.252
My name is Aryan, I'm here on behalf of Pillar New Zealand.

1534
01:30:03.292 --> 01:30:05.972
Pillar stands for Protecting Individual Life, Liberty and Rights.

1535
01:30:06.122 --> 01:30:09.612
We're a civil liberties outfit that believes that New Zealand has inherited a

1536
01:30:09.672 --> 01:30:13.252
wonderful heritage of democracy and liberty, and so we hope to preserve

1537
01:30:13.312 --> 01:30:16.532
that. From the outset, I'd like to

1538
01:30:17.152 --> 01:30:20.352
affirm that we're understanding of the motivations behind this bill and the goal

1539
01:30:20.392 --> 01:30:23.792
that is being pursued. Disorderly behavior in public that is left

1540
01:30:23.872 --> 01:30:24.492
unchallenged

1541
01:30:25.352 --> 01:30:29.342
reduces the public's enjoyment of public spaces and interferes with

1542
01:30:29.432 --> 01:30:33.241
commercial activity of local businesses and can instill a

1543
01:30:33.252 --> 01:30:36.652
sense of fear and danger in our urban areas, which affects, again, not just

1544
01:30:36.672 --> 01:30:39.832
businesses, but also families and individuals who are looking to

1545
01:30:40.472 --> 01:30:44.212
use and enjoy these spaces. Of course, safer central business

1546
01:30:44.252 --> 01:30:46.432
districts is a desirable goal to pursue.

1547
01:30:47.252 --> 01:30:50.512
However, the current wording of this legislation, we believe,

1548
01:30:51.112 --> 01:30:54.992
sets a poor legal precedent when it comes to the protection of civil liberties here

1549
01:30:55.012 --> 01:30:58.572
in New Zealand. Therefore, as sympathetic as we are to the goals being pursued,

1550
01:30:59.272 --> 01:31:01.112
we do not support the bill in its current form.

1551
01:31:02.292 --> 01:31:06.262
There are three first principles which guide our concerns and Also will

1552
01:31:06.262 --> 01:31:09.872
guide our recommendations, as you would've seen in the written

1553
01:31:09.932 --> 01:31:13.312
submission. Those are freedom of expression, freedom of movement, and right to

1554
01:31:13.352 --> 01:31:16.582
assembly, and legal proportionality and natural justice.

1555
01:31:16.672 --> 01:31:20.492
And that last point on legal proportionality is an issue that you'll see as

1556
01:31:20.672 --> 01:31:22.852
a running thread throughout our concerns.

1557
01:31:23.722 --> 01:31:27.532
Firstly, with regard to free speech, we're heartened by the protection

1558
01:31:27.692 --> 01:31:31.172
for protests. So we appreciate that, and I'm aware that that's in

1559
01:31:31.832 --> 01:31:35.452
the legislation. However, we are concerned by the proposed definition of

1560
01:31:35.532 --> 01:31:39.152
begging. As drafted, the bill extends beyond regulating

1561
01:31:39.252 --> 01:31:43.152
harmful conduct, and instead captures forms of peaceful expression,

1562
01:31:44.212 --> 01:31:46.942
including simply holding a sign or expressing personal need.

1563
01:31:47.652 --> 01:31:51.012
New Zealanders, including those who are socially or economically

1564
01:31:51.032 --> 01:31:52.142
deprived,

1565
01:31:53.012 --> 01:31:56.272
who would, for example, be beggars, have the right to express their need,

1566
01:31:56.952 --> 01:31:59.672
whether with speech or with writing on a sign.

1567
01:32:00.332 --> 01:32:03.832
Under the current provisions of this bill, begging alone, even passively with no

1568
01:32:03.952 --> 01:32:04.442
disruption,

1569
01:32:05.252 --> 01:32:07.462
could be legally punishable.

1570
01:32:08.432 --> 01:32:12.102
Now, with the current wording, we see ultimately the government arbitrating what

1571
01:32:12.152 --> 01:32:15.392
speech is acceptable within society and what speech is not acceptable.

1572
01:32:15.432 --> 01:32:17.892
That's the message I believe that is being sent.

1573
01:32:18.392 --> 01:32:20.692
And that's measured against its own political objectives.

1574
01:32:20.952 --> 01:32:24.832
As a matter of principle, this is a dangerous line of thinking to allow into the

1575
01:32:24.872 --> 01:32:26.132
legislative drafting

1576
01:32:27.272 --> 01:32:28.712
process that suggests where

1577
01:32:29.812 --> 01:32:33.572
speech and expression is an inconvenient obstacle to the

1578
01:32:33.592 --> 01:32:37.052
desired goals of the government, it can or even should be

1579
01:32:37.252 --> 01:32:37.812
suppressed.

1580
01:32:38.752 --> 01:32:42.451
So we believe that there's a very important and clear distinction between

1581
01:32:42.512 --> 01:32:46.442
conduct that is actually intimidating or interferes with others, and

1582
01:32:46.532 --> 01:32:49.432
speech that some may just find uncomfortable.

1583
01:32:49.632 --> 01:32:51.842
If we are to penalize anything, it ought to be activity,

1584
01:32:53.292 --> 01:32:56.232
not expression. And some might say, "Oh, look,

1585
01:32:56.792 --> 01:32:59.992
you're making a big deal out of this."

1586
01:33:00.972 --> 01:33:03.692
We believe that this is not the case.

1587
01:33:04.492 --> 01:33:08.352
The gang patch legislation that was passed earlier in this term, as an

1588
01:33:08.392 --> 01:33:12.312
example of even the attorney general agreeing that it was a suppression

1589
01:33:12.372 --> 01:33:16.171
on speech rights. And there are political arguments for and

1590
01:33:16.192 --> 01:33:19.672
against it, but we just see how this type of thinking can seep into the legislative

1591
01:33:19.752 --> 01:33:20.192
process.

1592
01:33:21.552 --> 01:33:25.352
And so again, we believe this is a slippery slope, and the

1593
01:33:25.372 --> 01:33:29.232
attorney general certainly, in his comments, has come to a similar conclusion

1594
01:33:29.272 --> 01:33:32.492
and believes that we can take a less rights intrusive approach

1595
01:33:33.332 --> 01:33:37.292
with regard to that. When it comes to freedom of movement and the

1596
01:33:37.332 --> 01:33:40.732
right to assembly, the legislation provides insufficient guidance to police

1597
01:33:40.792 --> 01:33:44.052
officers as to the geographical scope of a move on order.

1598
01:33:44.332 --> 01:33:47.752
That's not specified in the bill in its current writing at the moment, creating

1599
01:33:47.792 --> 01:33:51.452
uncertainty as to how far an individual may be required to leave a

1600
01:33:51.472 --> 01:33:54.932
particular location. For those experiencing homelessness or rough sleeping or

1601
01:33:54.952 --> 01:33:58.872
begging, being removed from an area may also mean being separated from

1602
01:33:58.912 --> 01:34:02.192
essential services or support that may be available nearby.

1603
01:34:02.772 --> 01:34:06.232
This doesn't mean that police should not have the power to intervene, obviously,

1604
01:34:06.832 --> 01:34:09.702
but rather where and when they do, their powers must be clearly defined,

1605
01:34:09.752 --> 01:34:13.711
proportionate, and at least considerate of the practical consequences of

1606
01:34:13.752 --> 01:34:17.272
displacement, including the individual's ability to access services.

1607
01:34:17.812 --> 01:34:21.692
Again, the current wording just doesn't achieve this

1608
01:34:21.732 --> 01:34:25.332
and doesn't do this. So again, as I said earlier, a less rights

1609
01:34:25.372 --> 01:34:29.292
infringing approach is possible, can be pursued, but

1610
01:34:29.332 --> 01:34:30.992
has not been done here,

1611
01:34:32.552 --> 01:34:33.852
and threaten civil liberties.

1612
01:34:34.712 --> 01:34:38.192
And finally, as I said, a running theme is that of legal proportionality and

1613
01:34:38.232 --> 01:34:38.912
natural justice.

1614
01:34:40.252 --> 01:34:43.892
This is most evidently clear in the penalties imposed by this

1615
01:34:43.952 --> 01:34:45.932
legislation for those in breach of move on orders.

1616
01:34:46.532 --> 01:34:50.521
Currently, the penalties for breaching an order sit alongside for penalties

1617
01:34:50.592 --> 01:34:54.402
for offenses that are significantly more serious, including

1618
01:34:54.652 --> 01:34:57.752
careless driving causing death or dealing class C drugs.

1619
01:34:59.592 --> 01:35:02.812
The regulatory impact statement shows that

1620
01:35:02.952 --> 01:35:06.452
of the options given to him, the minister has chosen the most aggressive

1621
01:35:06.512 --> 01:35:10.192
legislative path. We suggest that there are alternatives that can be

1622
01:35:10.212 --> 01:35:12.172
pursued and should be considered.

1623
01:35:12.961 --> 01:35:16.692
The sanctions proposed, in our view, are not proportionate to the conduct

1624
01:35:17.152 --> 01:35:20.792
being punished and exemplifies, again, a lack of constraint in

1625
01:35:20.832 --> 01:35:22.592
wielding the coercive power of the state.

1626
01:35:23.272 --> 01:35:23.592
Again,

1627
01:35:24.552 --> 01:35:28.412
you hear me saying this a lot, but there are less rights

1628
01:35:28.452 --> 01:35:31.391
infringing alternatives that can be pursued and should be pursued.

1629
01:35:32.312 --> 01:35:36.232
And so with that, look, my recommendations or our recommendations are

1630
01:35:36.242 --> 01:35:39.752
there. Much of it is around redrafting and using more clear terms,

1631
01:35:40.532 --> 01:35:44.222
and providing more guidance and just taking an approach that is more

1632
01:35:44.512 --> 01:35:48.472
proportionate when it comes to exercising or wielding the coercive power

1633
01:35:48.492 --> 01:35:48.912
of the state.

1634
01:35:49.832 --> 01:35:53.782
We ultimately believe that countries that value both liberty and democracy

1635
01:35:53.872 --> 01:35:56.872
have to be vigilant when it comes to wielding the coercive power of the state.

1636
01:35:57.732 --> 01:36:01.272
This bill, in its current form, fails that test of vigilance.

1637
01:36:02.212 --> 01:36:04.022
And so with that, I'm happy to take any questions.

1638
01:36:04.042 --> 01:36:06.112
We've got a couple questions for you, Aaron.

1639
01:36:06.512 --> 01:36:08.612
We'll start with Camilla, and then we'll go to Dan.

1640
01:36:10.712 --> 01:36:12.232
Thank you so much for your submission.

1641
01:36:12.272 --> 01:36:16.192
Apologies if I missed it, because I think we did a quick tiny power cut

1642
01:36:16.212 --> 01:36:16.832
here at Parliament.

1643
01:36:16.952 --> 01:36:16.962
Yeah.

1644
01:36:16.972 --> 01:36:20.572
But, I was really interested in the points that you made

1645
01:36:20.672 --> 01:36:24.552
around protests and how this potentially might affect

1646
01:36:25.092 --> 01:36:28.332
counter-protests. And so I just wondered if you could expand on that

1647
01:36:29.212 --> 01:36:32.912
slightly to make sure we can get some advice on your exact concern.

1648
01:36:33.672 --> 01:36:36.852
Yeah. Look, there is obviously that section that

1649
01:36:37.352 --> 01:36:39.472
provides protection for expressing ideas.

1650
01:36:40.152 --> 01:36:44.092
Where that gets a little bit murky is around what if there is someone who is

1651
01:36:44.312 --> 01:36:45.712
begging but also protesting

1652
01:36:46.772 --> 01:36:48.992
homelessness. That can be a bit murky.

1653
01:36:49.332 --> 01:36:52.712
But also there are certain terms here that are left undefined when it comes to

1654
01:36:52.972 --> 01:36:55.792
disorderly behavior or breaching the peace

1655
01:36:57.948 --> 01:37:01.698
If it is left undefined as it currently is in this

1656
01:37:01.728 --> 01:37:05.268
legislation, what happens is we have ultimately an arbitrary

1657
01:37:05.278 --> 01:37:09.208
application of the law and an imposition of a police officer in

1658
01:37:09.268 --> 01:37:12.458
Palmerston North might decide, "Actually, this is what breaching the peace is," and

1659
01:37:12.488 --> 01:37:15.688
a police officer in Auckland might decide that

1660
01:37:15.748 --> 01:37:19.588
this is what it is. And in both cases, we've seen in the past, there are

1661
01:37:20.168 --> 01:37:23.848
legal cases which are quite high profile, I won't name them here, I don't need to,

1662
01:37:23.968 --> 01:37:24.108
but

1663
01:37:25.348 --> 01:37:28.928
where someone holding a sign has been punished by police for

1664
01:37:29.008 --> 01:37:32.548
breaching peace, and those are actually going through the courts at the moment.

1665
01:37:32.608 --> 01:37:36.268
And so, again, it just comes to actually being vigilant when it comes to writing

1666
01:37:36.308 --> 01:37:38.668
legislation and being more considerate of these realities.

1667
01:37:39.448 --> 01:37:40.828
And so that's, yeah.

1668
01:37:42.108 --> 01:37:43.147
Dane Rosewarne, please.

1669
01:37:44.848 --> 01:37:48.528
Thanks for touching on the potential for a move on order,

1670
01:37:48.748 --> 01:37:52.428
like attracting penalties that are comparable to some much

1671
01:37:52.528 --> 01:37:56.228
more serious offenses. You're the first person that's actually raised that with the

1672
01:37:56.248 --> 01:37:56.668
committee

1673
01:37:57.588 --> 01:38:01.288
this morning, and I'll be keen to get some more advice from officials

1674
01:38:01.488 --> 01:38:05.448
on that. I'm just wondering if you could provide some more context

1675
01:38:05.508 --> 01:38:09.418
around why you consider those penalties disproportionate, and what

1676
01:38:09.468 --> 01:38:13.228
impact you think that that could actually have in practice.

1677
01:38:14.568 --> 01:38:15.688
Well, look,

1678
01:38:17.268 --> 01:38:20.268
one of the tests of good legislation is it's proportionate.

1679
01:38:21.048 --> 01:38:21.988
And that's not to say that

1680
01:38:22.908 --> 01:38:26.628
police shouldn't have the ability to exercise the punitive power of the

1681
01:38:26.708 --> 01:38:30.248
state. But at the moment, someone going to jail for

1682
01:38:30.288 --> 01:38:33.298
three months, which is one of the proposals under this

1683
01:38:33.328 --> 01:38:36.828
legislation, could happen for just holding up a sign

1684
01:38:37.448 --> 01:38:41.208
that's requesting money or begging as is the case

1685
01:38:41.268 --> 01:38:44.138
under section 8A (1)

1686
01:38:45.118 --> 01:38:48.908
. So look, the reality is that's just far too punitive, far too fast

1687
01:38:49.028 --> 01:38:50.968
for something that is far too little.

1688
01:38:53.288 --> 01:38:56.678
That's not to say that we can't ... The regulatory impact statement shows that

1689
01:38:56.728 --> 01:39:00.488
there are three other options that could be pursued with far less

1690
01:39:01.588 --> 01:39:02.108
imposition

1691
01:39:03.068 --> 01:39:06.468
from the state. And so I just think it's far too much for far too little.

1692
01:39:07.408 --> 01:39:08.528
Thank you. That's very helpful.

1693
01:39:08.608 --> 01:39:12.448
We've got one last question. We've only got about 45 seconds left, but Carl Bates,

1694
01:39:12.528 --> 01:39:12.848
please.

1695
01:39:13.388 --> 01:39:17.008
Thanks, Chair. I've got three quick parts to my question.

1696
01:39:17.128 --> 01:39:20.388
Why do you think submitters who've been submitting against us throughout the

1697
01:39:20.428 --> 01:39:24.168
morning have continually referenced the idea, like you just

1698
01:39:24.208 --> 01:39:26.668
have, that there would be three months

1699
01:39:27.728 --> 01:39:31.268
in prison for holding up a sign when that's not actually what the offense would be

1700
01:39:31.308 --> 01:39:35.108
for? Why do you think they ignore the role this gives police to

1701
01:39:35.168 --> 01:39:38.928
help people find the assistance they need, like the

1702
01:39:38.968 --> 01:39:42.928
crisis cafes? And C, why do you think submitters are not taking

1703
01:39:42.948 --> 01:39:46.788
into account or not commenting on the impact on families who

1704
01:39:46.868 --> 01:39:49.808
have small businesses that are currently being affected

1705
01:39:50.348 --> 01:39:54.088
by people begging at the doors of their

1706
01:39:54.188 --> 01:39:54.538
stores?

1707
01:39:55.368 --> 01:39:59.258
Look, I appreciate the question. I'm in no position to answer on behalf of

1708
01:39:59.288 --> 01:40:00.568
anyone else. Look,

1709
01:40:02.008 --> 01:40:05.457
the reality is these are our concerns, and we are appreciative of the fact that

1710
01:40:05.468 --> 01:40:07.548
businesses are impacted by this as well.

1711
01:40:07.568 --> 01:40:11.248
And so we do have to balance the rights of the different parties

1712
01:40:11.328 --> 01:40:15.148
here. The way we do that is through vigilance and through

1713
01:40:15.288 --> 01:40:17.988
good legal writing, and this bill does not do that.

1714
01:40:18.548 --> 01:40:19.748
I'm sorry, I won't be able to comment on-

1715
01:40:19.828 --> 01:40:20.288
No worries

1716
01:40:20.298 --> 01:40:23.608
... what others have said, but this is just the way it is at the moment. Thank you.

1717
01:40:23.648 --> 01:40:23.808
Okay.

1718
01:40:23.868 --> 01:40:27.828
Thank you very much, Aaron, and for Pillar's submission.

1719
01:40:28.268 --> 01:40:31.388
You've been submitting to a number of bills, and they've been very well,

1720
01:40:32.328 --> 01:40:35.228
the way you're setting them out, so well done on that.

1721
01:40:35.348 --> 01:40:37.068
So thank you for your time today.

1722
01:40:37.648 --> 01:40:38.648
Awesome. Thank you so much, guys.

